\title{baux}
\author{Kvutza}
\date{2026-11-12}
\meta{external}{https://github.com/Kvutza/baux}

\p{
  \strong{baux} is a black box optimization library for expensive or noisy functions #{f(x)} evaluated over small sample budgets, from low-dimensional design parameters up to #{10^5}-weight control policies. At each query #{x}, Epistemic Nearest Neighbors (Bafna, Jadhav, & Sweet [[BJS25]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.12818); [yubo-research/enn](https://github.com/yubo-research/enn)) predicts value and uncertainty from the #{K} closest evaluated points weighted by inverse variance, avoiding global #{N \times N} covariance matrix inversion as observations accumulate.
}

\p{
  For high-dimensional or multimodal objectives, \strong{baux} runs parallel local trust regions (Eriksson et al. [[EPG+19]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01739)) that perturb sparse coordinate subsets around regional incumbents. Observation arrays, regional side lengths and counters, pending asynchronous tickets, and a splittable 128-bit PRNG key live in an immutable state object advanced by pure functions ([API Reference](docs.html#baux.init)).
}

\p{
  A basic loop pairs an \code{ENN} surrogate ([baux.enn](docs.html#baux.enn)) with upper-confidence acquisition ([baux.ucb](docs.html#baux.ucb)) and updates state via [baux.init](docs.html#baux.init), [baux.ask](docs.html#baux.ask), and [baux.tell](docs.html#baux.tell):
}

\<div>[id]{quickstart}{
\pre{
import numpy as np
import baux


def objective(x):
    return -np.sum((x - 0.4) ** 2, axis=1)


bounds = np.tile([-2.0, 2.0], (6, 1))
pipe = baux.enn(k=10) | baux.ucb(beta=2.0)

state = baux.init(bounds, pipe, key=baux.key(0))
for _ in range(15):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, num_arms=4)
    state, _ = baux.tell(state, batch, objective(batch.points))

assert state.best_x.shape == (6,) and state.best_y > -0.25
}
}

